5.text:
The fixed positive-strand asymmetry restricts recombination to one consistent direction per meiosis, but does not eliminate any of the enumerated patterns reachable after exactly two single COs. Each meiosis still permits a choice of which parental homolog supplies the prefix (i.e., the “starting allele” at the first SNP), so every haplotype whose transition count is at most 2 remains attainable: the two pure parentals (via cancellation), all eight single-transition strings (via recovery), and all twelve double-transition strings (via augmentation at any pair of distinct intervals). No haplotype with three or more transitions can be produced, because that would require at least three CO events across the two-generation pedigree. Consequently the reachable set on the positive strand is exactly the 22 strings of length 5 that contain 0, 1 or 2 transitions.

The 16-count arguments rest on an inconsistent model: they treat the four intervals as receiving independent binary assignments after only two COs, which is impossible under the single-Crossover-per-meiosis rule. The interval-mask construction therefore both under- and over-counts and cannot be reconciled with the pedigree constraints.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

